! R23-9694 (7030 CE Introductory Programming, Vol. 2) printed p.82,
! the Programming Example under STORE ROOT, transcribed statement for
! statement.  The page's own words:
!
!   "Assume a short routine is to check the operation of the
!   instructions MULTIPLY, DIVIDE, and STORE ROOT.  One method is to
!   multiply a number by itself to form the square of the number, then
!   divide the square of the number by the original number and compare
!   the result with the result of a STORE ROOT instruction.  Assume
!   the original number, at location NUMBER, is positive and has an
!   exponent well within the N range.  The program is:"
!
!     Name     Instruction        Remarks
!     PREP     LCI, $X1, 200.0    Routine to be executed 200 times.
!     START    L(N), NUMBER       Place number in accumulator.
!              *(N), NUMBER       Square the number.
!              SRT (N), TEMP      Store root of square in TEMP.
!              /(N), NUMBER       Divide square by number.
!     CHECK    K(U), TEMP         Compare root with quotient; should
!                                 be equal.
!              BZAE, ERROR        If not equal, branch to error routine.
!     RETURN   CB, $X1, START     Repeat 199 times
!     NEXT     First Instruction of next routine
!
! THIS IS A SELF-CHECKING PROGRAM, which is a different kind of oracle
! from anything else in this suite.  No expected value had to be
! derived or transcribed: IBM wrote the comparison and the error
! branch, and the test is simply whether 200 iterations reach NEXT
! rather than ERROR.  sqrt(x*x) and (x*x)/x must agree, 200 times
! over, through MULTIPLY, STORE ROOT, DIVIDE and COMPARE.
!
! NUMBER IS 3.0, chosen to satisfy the page's two conditions --
! positive, exponent well within the N range -- and so that every
! intermediate is exact in the 48-bit fraction: 3, 9, sqrt(9) = 3,
! 9/3 = 3.  An inexact choice would be testing the rounding rules
! rather than the four instructions, and the page is not about
! rounding.  Encoded by tests/fp_arith_expected.py's `enc`, the same
! routine the FP decks use: Reference Manual pp.78-79, bit 0 EF, 1-10
! EM, 11 ES, 12-59 fraction, 60 sign.  3.0 is fraction 0.75 with
! exponent 2.
!
! WHAT EACH OUTCOME MEANS
!   PC at NEXT   -- 200 iterations agreed.  The intended pass.
!   PC at ERROR  -- some iteration disagreed; MULTIPLY, STORE ROOT,
!                   DIVIDE or COMPARE is wrong.  IBM's own verdict.
!   TEMP = 3.0   -- checked as well as the branch, because a STORE
!                   ROOT that stored nothing at all would leave TEMP
!                   holding its initial value and the comparison
!                   would then be against whatever the quotient was.
!                   Checking the branch alone would pass on a
!                   coincidence; checking TEMP says the root was
!                   actually computed and stored.

        SLC, (8)2000.0

PREP    LCI, $X1, 200.0       ! routine to be executed 200 times
START   L(N), NUMBER          ! place number in accumulator
        *(N), NUMBER          ! square the number
        SRT(N), TEMP          ! store root of square in TEMP
        /(N), NUMBER          ! divide square by number
CHECK   K(U), TEMP            ! compare root with quotient; equal
        BZAE, ERROR           ! if not equal, branch to error routine
RETURN  CB, $X1, START        ! repeat 199 times
NEXT    BEW, $                ! "first instruction of next routine"
ERROR   BEW, $                ! IBM's error routine, as a halt

! Data, word-aligned: BEW is a half word, so without this the 64-bit
! fields below would straddle two words.
        SLC, (8)2020.0

! 3.0 -- fraction 0.75, exponent 2, positive.
NUMBER  DD(BU,64,8), (8)0001140000000000000000
TEMP    DD(BU,64,8), 0

        END, PREP
